#
# 1. query_orders(t1, t2) in the original was a bare stub (`return`).
#    This version calls run_data_intake_pipeline instead, which already
#    contains the real Oracle query.
#
# 2. This fills FORWARD from the newest record only. Interior gaps --
#    from, say, a run that crashed midweek -- are invisible to it.
#    Detecting those needs a per-window presence check:
#        select distinct date_trunc('hour', wr_status_upd_dttm) from orders
#    Fine to leave out under the immutable-record assumption, but worth
#    raising.
#
# 3. Late-arriving records: if a work order lands in the source system
#    after its own timestamp, its window may already be behind max_dttm
#    and get skipped. The floor() change above helps for the current
#    partial window. A fuller fix is re-pulling the last 24-48 hours on
#    every run and letting the dedupe guard absorb the overlap.
#
# 4. Cold start = a full retention period of hourly windows (~720 pulls).
#    May want to cap or batch that for the first run.
#
# 5. cleaned_text retention -- see note in manage_retention.
#
# 6. Config keys: confirm whether settings.etl has window_size,
#    window_hours, or both. This file uses window_size throughout.
